@packages/compiler import { SigilAgentAdapter, SigilAgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator, SigilAgentAdapterSubprocess, SigilAgentEvaluationRequest }

component SigilCodexCompilerAdapter {
  goal {
    Execute provider-neutral compiler evaluation through the Codex CLI and return
    one validated result under an ephemeral, read-only inspection policy.
  }

  interface {
    CodexAdapter {
      evaluate(SigilAgentEvaluationRequest) implements SigilAgentAdapter for provider
      codex with implementation identity builtin.codex-cli and the exact adapter
      package version

      provider-specific declarations match capabilities schemaVersion 1 with
      read-only workspace access, no agent-tool network, no approval escalation, and
      ephemeral state persistence.

      Observability declares no progress, final usage, unavailable cost, and
      post-settlement-only token budget enforcement with unavailable cost
      enforcement.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilCodexCompilerAdapter {
  logic {
    CodexAdapter {
      Consume the provider-neutral request validation and capability-match result
      before invocation. Write the findings schema to a temporary file, then invoke
      the Codex CLI with that schema, a never approval policy, disabled web search,
      the optional selected model, and the prompt on standard input.

      The prompt carries evaluationPrompt output unchanged. That provider-neutral
      envelope preserves stage, target identity, purpose, selected retrieval
      evidence, evaluator guidance, command policy, findings schema, and result
      instructions; capability, budget, and snapshot fields remain control metadata.

      Supply Codex execution and cleanup hooks to
      SigilAgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator and use SigilAgentAdapterSubprocess for
      the private process lifecycle. Parse the line-delimited event stream and
      require exactly one completed turn carrying a findings JSON object.

      Preserve standard-error content on a process failure so an invocation failure
      explains itself.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    CodexExecutionBoundary {
      Schema-valid agent messages inside one open turn are nonterminal checkpoints.
      The latest checkpoint becomes that turn's candidate result only when
      turn.completed arrives.

      A second completed turn candidate, or any framing after the completed terminal
      boundary, rejects as final-result-protocol.

      An event line exceeding the provider frame limit, an event line that is not
      valid JSON, a stream without a final agent message, and a terminal result
      exceeding the final-result limit each reject as final-result-protocol.

      The temporary schema file is removed after invocation, and a schema file that
      was not created rejects before the provider starts.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CheckpointedTurnTerminal {
      Decision: Treat schema-valid agent messages as nonterminal checkpoints and
      settle the terminal result only on turn.completed.

      Scope: Governs Codex event-stream parsing; excludes request validation and
      subprocess cleanup.

      Design issues addressed: A Codex turn can emit several schema-valid messages
      before it completes, so accepting the first would settle on a partial result.

      Consequences: The last checkpoint of the completed turn is authoritative, and
      framing after that boundary is a protocol failure rather than a later result.
    }

    SchemaFileHandoff {
      Decision: Pass the findings schema to the Codex CLI through a temporary file
      rather than inline argument content.

      Scope: Governs Codex invocation; excludes the schema's shape, which the
      provider-neutral request owns.

      Trade-offs: The adapter owns a temporary file lifecycle, including removal
      after a failed invocation.

      Consequences: Schema size does not enter the command line, and a missing schema
      file fails before the provider starts.
    }
  }

  cases {
    CodexAdapter {
      An ephemeral request invokes the Codex CLI once and accepts the completed
      turn's last checkpoint after process and output settlement.

      A persistent request mismatches before invocation.

      A stream with no final agent message, a non-JSON event line, an oversized event
      line, and framing after the completed boundary are final-result-protocol.

      Timeout, cancellation, or process failure follows bounded subprocess cleanup
      and preserves standard-error content.
    }
  }
}
